//? Title: Diffie–Hellman: Agreement, Exact Arithmetic and What the Transcript Does Not Prove
//? Subtitle: Retain both original exchanges, inspect actual exponent bits, and expose small-group structure and unauthenticated substitution
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Two parties want to compute the same value while exchanging only public
messages. They need an algebraic agreement rule, and they must distinguish
that rule from secrecy and peer authentication. Finite-field Diffie–Hellman
uses modular powers so that both parties obtain g^(ab) modulo p. We retain
the original p=23 example and the larger 31-bit exchange, then actually
recover the latter's sample exponent with an existing core discrete-log
solver. Native traces expose square-and-multiply states and exact operation
counts. Bounded group cycles show periods and tiny subgroups rather than
using a scattered plot as evidence of hardness. A two-session substitution
demonstrates the missing authentication. All computations use the canonical
uint64 core; these reproducible, variable-time examples are arithmetic
illustrations, not a secure key-agreement implementation.
)

\toc

> The original agreement, with visible public messages

Take p=23, g=5, Alice's private exponent a=6 and Bob's b=15. Alice publishes
A=5^6 mod 23=8; Bob publishes B=5^15 mod 23=19. Alice computes B^6 mod 23=2,
and Bob computes A^15 mod 23=2. The exponents are shown here so the worked
example can be checked; an actual protocol keeps them private.

For a prime p and a nonzero group element g,

$$
B^a=(g^b)^a=g^{ab}=(g^a)^b=A^b\pmod p.
$$
\alttext(B raised to a equals g raised to b times a, which equals g raised to a times b and A raised to b, modulo p.)

The identity establishes agreement. It does not establish that an observer
cannot compute the value or that either peer has the expected identity.
Recovering a discrete logarithm suffices to break the exchange; the passive
computational target is the shared value given g, p, A and B. We do not infer
that recovering an exponent is the only possible route to that target.
[Diffie and Hellman's original paper](https://web.mit.edu/6.857/OldStuff/Fall03/ref/diffie76new.pdf)
itself distinguishes the known direction from its unproved converse.

The program also retains the original 31-bit prime p=2^31−1 with g=7.
The same public values and shared residue reappear. The existing
baby_step_giant_step_trace() takes only that public generator, modulus and
Alice's public value, recovers the sample exponent, and recomputes the shared
value. No private exponent is passed to the recovery call.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dh_exchange.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto x=dm::algorithms::dh_exchange(5,6,15,23),large=dm::algorithms::dh_exchange(7,123456,987654,2147483647);
  if(x.alice_public!=8 || x.bob_public!=19 || x.alice_shared!=2 || x.bob_shared!=2 || large.alice_shared!=large.bob_shared)return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.5;box.height=2;box.font_size=23;
  d.node("alice",{0,0},std::format("Alice: a = 6\nPublish A = {}\nB^a mod p = {}",x.alice_public,x.alice_shared),box)
   .node("bob",{8,0},std::format("Bob: b = 15\nPublish B = {}\nA^b mod p = {}",x.bob_public,x.bob_shared),box)
   .edge("alice","bob",std::format("A = {}",x.alice_public)).edge("bob","alice",std::format("B = {}",x.bob_public))
   .text({4,2.4},"Public parameters p = 23, g = 5; agreed raw residue = 2",24)
   .text({4,-2.4},"The messages carry A and B; the private exponents are displayed only for this example",20)
   .save_svg("build/dh_exchange.svg");
  std::print("| original exchange | p | g | Alice public | Bob public | Alice raw shared | Bob raw shared |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| textbook | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",x.modulus,x.generator,x.alice_public,x.bob_public,x.alice_shared,x.bob_shared);
  std::print("| 31-bit prime | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n\n",large.modulus,large.generator,large.alice_public,large.bob_public,large.alice_shared,large.bob_shared);
  const auto recovered=dm::algorithms::baby_step_giant_step_trace(large.generator,large.alice_public,large.modulus);
  if(!recovered.exists || dm::algorithms::dh_public_key(large.generator,recovered.exponent,large.modulus)!=large.alice_public)return 1;
  const auto shared=dm::algorithms::dh_shared_secret(large.bob_public,recovered.exponent,large.modulus);
  if(shared!=large.alice_shared)return 1;
  std::print("Public-input recovery on the 31-bit example: exponent {}; baby products {}; giant lookups {}; recomputed shared {}.\n",recovered.exponent,recovered.baby_steps,recovered.giant_steps,shared);
}
