\caption(Both original exchanges reproduce their exact values. The diagram distinguishes public messages from the private exponents displayed for the toy calculation; a public-input search also breaks the 31-bit sample.)
\alttext(Alice and Bob boxes show private exponents six and fifteen, outgoing public values eight and nineteen, and equal raw shared residue two. Opposite arrows carry the two public values.)

> Follow the actual square-and-multiply states

Write e as a sum of binary powers. At each low-bit-first step, the running
base represents g^(2^i) modulo m. When bit i is set, multiply it into the
accumulator; then square the running base and shift the remaining exponent.
The invariant is

$$
\text{accumulator}\times\text{running}^{\text{remaining}}
\equiv g^e\pmod m.
$$
\alttext(The accumulator times the running base raised to the remaining exponent is congruent to the original power g to e modulo m.)

For e=6, binary110, the low bits are zero, one, one. The running residues
are 5,2,4 and the accumulator becomes 1,2,8. The existing loop also computes
a final, unused running-base square 16. The trace retains that actual work;
it does not silently substitute an optimized loop. Raising Bob's public 19
to the same exponent instead ends at the shared residue 2.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dh_bit_trace.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::mod_pow_trace(5,6,23),shared=dm::algorithms::mod_pow_trace(19,6,23);
  if(t.result!=8 || shared.result!=2 || t.work.products!=5 || t.steps.back().squared_base!=16)return 1;
  std::print("| bit index | remaining exponent | multiply | running before | accumulator before | accumulator after | squared base |\n| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> bits,running,accumulator,squares;
  for(const auto& s:t.steps) {
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",s.bit_index,s.remaining_before,s.multiply,s.running_before,s.accumulator_before,s.accumulator_after,s.squared_base);
    bits.push_back(s.bit_index);running.push_back(s.running_before);accumulator.push_back(s.accumulator_after);squares.push_back(s.squared_base);
  }
  std::print("\nPublic power result {}; shared power result {}; actual squares {}; accumulator products {}; total products {}.\n",t.result,shared.result,t.work.squarings,t.work.accumulator_products,t.work.products);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.line(bits,running,"running before",{37,99,235},2).points(bits,running,"running before",{37,99,235},5)
   .line(bits,accumulator,"accumulator after",{22,163,74},2).points(bits,accumulator,"accumulator after",{22,163,74},5)
   .line(bits,squares,"squared base",{124,58,237},2).points(bits,squares,"squared base",{124,58,237},5)
   .legend({"running before","accumulator after","squared base"},{{37,99,235},{22,163,74},{124,58,237}})
   .title("Actual low-bit-first states for 5^6 modulo 23").x_label("processed bit index, least significant first")
   .y_label("exact residue").x_ticks({0,1,2},{"0: skip","1: multiply","2: multiply"}).y_limits(0,26).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/dh_bit_trace.svg");
}
