| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 6 | false | 5 | 1 | 1 | 2 |
| 1 | 3 | true | 2 | 1 | 2 | 4 |
| 2 | 1 | true | 4 | 2 | 8 | 16 |

Public power result 8; shared power result 2; actual squares 3; accumulator products 2; total products 5.
\image(figures/dh_bit_trace.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The complete actual trace includes the skipped first multiply and the final unused square. Three squares plus two accumulator products give five modular products.)
\alttext(Three labelled residue curves show running bases five-two-four, accumulators one-two-eight and squared bases two-four-sixteen across the three processed exponent bits.)

The full product of two uint64 values fits in unsigned 128 bits. Reducing that
complete product avoids an intermediate 64-bit wrap. This is exact integer
arithmetic on the supported compiler, including moduli near 2^64; it says
nothing about secrecy. The scalar helpers and trace share one kernel.

> A small power plot has visible group structure

For an element of order r, g^(x+r)=g^x. The full generator 5 modulo23 has
order 22: exponents 0 through 21 enumerate all nonzero residues exactly once.
The original plot used 0 through 22 and therefore included the identity twice.
With g=4, the order is11; with g=22 it is2; g=1 has order 1. These are exact
periods, not evidence of random behavior. A tiny table immediately inverts
the original public values 8 and 19 to exponents 6 and 15 modulo 22.

The bounded cycle API validates primality and the element range, then emits
actual successive powers until the first return to one. Its quoted cycle
products exclude the separate primality check. Large groups need a different
order-analysis strategy; the default cap prevents unbounded enumeration.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dh_cycles.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| element g | exact order | enumeration products | first residue | last before identity repeats |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::uint64_t g:{1,4,5,22}) {
    const auto c=dm::algorithms::dh_power_cycle(g,23);std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",g,c.order,c.order,c.values.front(),c.values.back());
  }
  const auto full=dm::algorithms::dh_power_cycle(5,23),sub=dm::algorithms::dh_power_cycle(4,23);
  const auto a=std::find(full.values.begin(),full.values.end(),8)-full.values.begin(),b=std::find(full.values.begin(),full.values.end(),19)-full.values.begin();
  if(a!=6 || b!=15 || full.order!=22 || sub.order!=11)return 1;
  std::print("\nSmall-table inverse: public 8 gives exponent {}; public 19 gives exponent {}. Exponents 6 and 28 both publish {}.\n\n",a,b,dm::algorithms::dh_public_key(5,28,23));
  std::vector<double> xs,five,four;std::print("| exponent x | 5^x mod 23 | 4^x mod 23 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t x=0;x<=22;++x) {
    xs.push_back(x);five.push_back(full.values[x%full.values.size()]);four.push_back(sub.values[x%sub.values.size()]);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",x,full.values[x%full.values.size()],sub.values[x%sub.values.size()]);
  }
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.points(xs,five,"g=5",{37,99,235},5).title("Full order 22; identity repeats at x=22")
   .x_label("integer exponent x").y_label("5^x modulo23").y_limits(0,26);
  auto q=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();q.points(xs,four,"g=4",{234,88,12},5).title("Subgroup order 11; the period repeats twice")
   .x_label("integer exponent x").y_label("4^x modulo23").y_limits(0,26);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(p).add(q).size(1100,520).title("Power maps are periodic group maps, not a security test").fit_panels().save_svg("build/dh_cycles.svg");
}
