> Contracts and verification

The established dh_public_key(), dh_shared_secret(), mod_pow() and mul_mod()
names remain available through the original include. The power wrappers
retain arithmetic-only behavior, including composite moduli, zero exponents,
zero bases and modulus one. The modular product now rejects a zero modulus
before remainder evaluation. These raw APIs do not sample private exponents,
validate a protocol group or peer, authenticate messages, supply a KDF or
provide constant-time arithmetic. The trace deliberately exposes exponent
bits. Matching residues on p=1 or on a composite modulus are algebraic
results, not a validated prime-field agreement.

A complete effective bit count is checked before trace allocation/powering;
its default cap is 64, and modulus one has zero effective bits. The cycle
API separately checks a prime p>=2 and 1<=g<p, then applies an online default
one-million-value cap. It emits the identity once and performs a closing
product; enumeration costs O(order) storage/products plus its primality
check. Zero disables only optional caps; vector capacity checks remain.
The scalar powering driver uses O(1) storage and O(log(e)+1) fixed-word
operations; traces own O(log(e)+1) records. Multiprecision bit-operation cost
is not measured here. No random dataset fits these exact finite controls.

Nine native tests check 72,900 small exchanges, including composite rings,
and 103,275 complete small power traces against repeated multiplication and
the state invariant. Addition-only multiplication with an independent
left-to-right power driver checks 5,000 full-width products and 500 powers.
Every nonzero element of every prime through 127 is enumerated independently;
a full-width prime's order-two element tests the closing product near 2^64.
An optional Python development oracle separately verifies 20,000 arbitrary-
precision product/power controls, including zero and full-width moduli.
Original values, periods, subgroup collapse, two-session substitution,
complete trace caps, online cycle caps, edge conventions and owned copies
are checked. These validations establish arithmetic and report behavior;
they do not turn the toy implementation into a secure cryptographic protocol.

> References

1. W. Diffie and M. E. Hellman (1976), “New Directions in Cryptography”, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 22(6), 644–654. [Primary paper](https://web.mit.edu/6.857/OldStuff/Fall03/ref/diffie76new.pdf), [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1976.1055638). Algebraic agreement, repeated squaring and the distinction between logarithm recovery and the shared-value target.
2. D. Gillmor (2016), RFC 7919, “Negotiated Finite Field Diffie-Hellman Ephemeral Parameters for Transport Layer Security (TLS)”. [Primary specification, section 5.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7919.html#section-5.1). Peer checks in the context of its specified groups; this report's raw arithmetic is not an implementation of the RFC.
